problem_statement:
Derive formulas and calculate the values of the following quantities for the Moon. Use the definitions and data below, and report numerical results in SI units. Definitions: G is the universal gravitational constant, M is the mass of the Moon, R is the radius of the Moon, m is the mass of a test body at the Moon's surface, g is the gravitational acceleration at the Moon's surface, v_0 is the escape velocity from the Moon, E is the total mechanical energy of the projectile, U(ρ) is the gravitational potential energy at radial distance ρ from the Moon's center, and ρ is the radial distance from the Moon's center. Numerical data to use: G = 6.67×10^{-11} \unit{m^3/(kg s^2)}, M = 7.35×10^{22} \unit{kg}, R = 1.74×10^{6} \unit{m}

a) the gravitational acceleration at the surface of the Moon

b) the escape velocity from the Moon.

Two concentric metal spheres of radii a and b with a<b are separated by a medium of permittivity ε and conductivity σ. At time t=0 an electric charge q is suddenly placed on the inner sphere. Use the radial coordinate r with a<r<b. Let E(t,r) denote the electric field intensity in the medium, j(t,r) the current density, and I(t) the total current through any concentric spherical surface of radius r with a<r<b. Let w(t,r) be the Joule heat power density, W the total Joule heat produced, and W0 the initial electrostatic energy stored in the medium. SI units: a,b,r in \unit{m}, t in \unit{s}, ε in \unit{F/m}, σ in \unit{S/m}, q in \unit{C}, E in \unit{V/m}, j in \unit{A/m^2}, I in \unit{A}, w in \unit{W/m^3}, and W,W0 in \unit{J}

a) Calculate the total current I(t) through the medium as a function of time.

b) Calculate the total Joule heat W produced by this current and show that it equals the decrease in electrostatic energy W0 that occurs as a consequence of the rearrangement of the charge.

A discriminator using a tunnel diode can be designed to operate with a very low threshold. Let V_{th,min} denote the minimum achievable threshold voltage. Choose the correct value from options a–c.

a) 1 \unit{mV}

b) 10 \unit{mV}

c) 100 \unit{mV}

Assume monochromatic Fraunhofer diffraction by a single slit or a rectangular aperture of width w measured perpendicular to the direction in which resolution is required. Let lambda denote the wavelength of light, theta the diffraction angle from the central maximum to the first minimum, and Delta the path difference between rays from the extreme top and bottom edges of the aperture at observation angle theta. Assume w >> lambda so the small-angle approximation sin theta = theta (in radians) is valid. For part (b), let s be the linear separation between two parallel lines on the lunar surface, D the distance from the observer to the Moon, and alpha the corresponding angular separation as seen by the telescope. Use the single-slit first-minimum condition as the resolution criterion, and identify the required aperture width with w.

s) Show that for Fraunhofer diffraction by a slit the direction of the first minimum on either side of the central maximum is given by theta = lambda / w, where w is the width of the slit and w >> lambda

b) How wide must a rectangular aperture in front of a telescope be in order to resolve parallel lines one kilometer apart on the surface of the Moon? Assume the light has wavelength lambda = 500 \times 10^{-9} \unit{m} and the distance of the Moon is 400000 \unit{km}

Estimate the intensity of moonlight on Earth. Let I_m denote the average moonlight intensity at Earth's surface during full Moon, expressed in \unit{W cm^{-2}}. For part (b), let E_{ph} denote the energy per photon, h the Planck constant, \nu the photon frequency, c the speed of light, \lambda a representative visible wavelength, and N the visible-band photon flux at Earth's surface in \unit{cm^{-2} s^{-1}}.

a) In \unit{W cm^{-2}}

b) In photons per \unit{cm^{-2} s^{-1}} (in the visible range).

