problem_statement:
Read the following problem and provide your answer.

a) Proton finite-size shift in hydrogen. Assume the proton is a uniformly charged sphere of radius R with R much smaller than the Bohr radius a_0. Let e denote the elementary charge in Gaussian units so that the point-Coulomb potential is e^2 r^{-1}. Let Ψ_{nlm}(r,θ,φ) be the normalized hydrogenic wave function, and let Ψ_{ns}(0) denote the s-state value at the origin. Let Y_{00} be the l=0 spherical harmonic with Y_{00}=1/√(4π). Let ħ be the reduced Planck constant, c the speed of light, m the electron mass, and α the fine-structure constant α=e^2(ħ c)^{-1}. Show that the first-order energy shift of an s-wave level relative to the point-proton case is approximately $$
\Delta E_{ns}\approx\frac{2\pi}{5} e^{2} |\Psi_{ns}(0)|^{2} R^{2}
$$ Explain why the shift is much smaller for non-s states. The normalized 2s hydrogenic wave function is $$
\Psi_{2s}(r)= (2a_{0})^{-3/2} \pi^{-1/2} \bigg(1-\frac{r}{2a_{0}}\bigg) \exp\bigg(-\frac{r}{2a_{0}}\bigg)
$$ Using $$
a_{0}\approx 5\times 10^{-9} \unit{cm}
$$ and $$
R\approx 10^{-13} \unit{cm}
$$ find the approximate 2s–2p splitting induced by this effect. Define the requested final numerical answer as $$
\Delta E_{\text{split}}=E_{2s}-E_{2p}
$$ and report it in \unit{eV}.

